Hello, I will present a new tool, which I am developing.
This tool is for finding and fixing unused Python code. 

My presentation consists out of four parts.
Firstly, I will describe current state of unused code detection using existing tools.
Secondly, I will present my deadcoded package and its advantages.
Thirdly, I will show how to use it practically.
Finally, I will compare several deadcode detection strategies.

Let's start with defining what dead code is, because there are a lot of different definitions.
I will define dead code as unused, no longer needed code, that had to be removed, but it was not.

This type of unused code is actually a problem,
because it creates technical debt.
> It requires cognitive capacity to understand it.
> Also, it has to be maintained, because whenever dependency or python nversion gets updated,
you have to update all your code base, including unused code.
Hence, it consumes time.
> Also, unused code might use dependencies,
that might get outdated and might have security vulnerabilities.
Unused code might even increase security risks.

Unused code is like evil agents from the Matrix,
who are waiting to cause you trouble.

We actually want to get rid of unused code. 
And removing dead code saves time, money and reduces security risks.


I have a short story to tell.
Once, I have bumped into a Pull request with a new feature.
which contained an unused class.
Author intially wanted to use it, but decided not to and forgot to 
remove it.
As a result that unused class remained in the pull request.

I was surprised that this type of unused code was not detected by our linters,
because we used bleeding edge ones.
We were using ruff with all linting rules enabled.
And this type of unused code slipped into our Pull request - undetected.

It would be excellent to detect and automatically remove
such unutilised code occourencies.

This idea led me to an investigation: why ruff was
not able to detect this type of unuse code.
I have reviewed over 700 linting rules of Ruff
and found out that there are only several rules for unused code.
They are for local unused code: basically for local variables, arguments and imports.

This is only a tiny fraction of all unused code,
which might exist in a code base.
Ruff is not capable to detect globally unused code yet.

I searched for other existing tools.
And found that vulture is the most popular and capable linter
which deals with such kind of unused code cases.

It is the most popular option with over three thousand github stars, it is old and mature.

I tried using it, but I have faced several issues.

Lets consider this example.
We have a class with several attributes and
this class has a nested class called Meta, 
that is commonly used for configuration.

Vulture package has detected a lot of false positives in this case.
It provided each attribute and option as a finding of unused code.

For larger code base it is very time consuming and inconvenient
to list all these False positives case by case.
If i wanted to add a new attribute,
I would have to add it's name to vulture configuration.

This is really time consuming and inconvenient.
There is no way to tune out these False positives.
This made vulture unusable in my case.

Another huge disadvantage of vulture is that you have to remove unsued 
code items manually. Vulture is only capable to find and reporting them.
you have to manually remove these entries yourself manually.


I have considered improving Vulture,
but it seemed that the refactoring needed was too significant.
so I decided to create a new tool for deadcode detection, not to improve the existing one.
That is how deadcode package was born.

Deadcode is implemented in Python, but I think eventually it might be 
reimplemented in Rust and integrated into Ruff,
if it gets recognised.

Comparing this deadcode package with vulture: I see three main
advantages why deadcode is better and these three advantages are like:

Deadcode provides way more options to tune out false positives.
Its more flexible.

Second advantage it is able to find more cases of unused code.
And finally it provides automatic fixing feature, which allows to 
automatically remove all the finding without doing any manual job.


lets look into these features in more detail.
First feature, which allows to tune out false possitives is implemented
by adding more options to ignore code in different ways.

So I have used three terms to identify what has to be ignored.
And These terms are name, body and definition.

You can see in this example. Those code parts are marked accordingly.
So by providing name you can choose what to get ignore,
single name, body or a whole definition.

That adds a lot of flexibility. You dont have to specify name 
each time you have a new occourance.


And in addition to a way to ignore code by name, body and definition.
Those options also allow to ignore code based on usage.
So, You can specify base class of which all child classes have to be ignored.

Or you can specify a decorator with which code item is decorated and based
on that code might be filtered out.

Looking into this example: we can see, that a single entry ignore
definitions 
allow to ignore all meta classes 
And we dont have to list options explicitly anymore.
This applies for decorators, base classes.

Another advantage is that deadcode provides more types of checks.
And all of these checks might be ignore per line or per file.
as provided in any of these examples. Those checks are mostly 
similar to vultures checks, a couple addictional were added.
For empty Python code, unreachable code.

And last advantage is automatic fixing. 
It is pretty convenient to automatically fix code, but sometimes
we want to make sure that this code is being removed correctly.
And we can see that by using additional --dry flag.

Also another interesting feature is sometimes deadcode might
want to remove all content of a file. In such cases deadcode
will remove the file it self, unless it is a special file.
Which is named using dunder naming.



Lets see, how this package might be used.
It is pretty straight forward.
You just have to write, pip install deadcode.
And you are ready to use it.
After the installation you have to run a CLI command line tool.
    and provide a list of files, that have to be checked.

If you want to check all the files in a python directory. You can use a dot.

Lets consider this simple example, which.


It contains a lot of unused code entries.
    And these unused code entries are highlighted in purple. 

When you run deadcode: you get a list of unused code entries.
    And if you want you can automatically fix it by using --fix option.

    In this case we see, that the same list of findings is provided.
    We get additional note, that 7 unused code items were automatically removed.

Here is a resulting file, which we get after running this automatic checking.
We see that this was pretty convenient.
    removing those unused code entries automatically.


Yea, it is really important to highlight, that we have to tune out
all false positives, before using automatic code removal, because
good working code might suffer, so we should make our best to make sure
that deadcode configuration is correct and findings are valid.

And only then we have to run that automatic fixing.




And ehh lets move to the last part, lets compare different
strategies of detecting unused code. To begin with: it is
I would like to highligh that detecting unused code
is really complex task, because there is no way to associate
whole definition with its usage.

Both vulture, and deadcode package are using abstratct syntax tree
to partially parse python files, but their result is a tree of names,
and there is no easy ways to associate the definition with the places
where those names are being used.

Even though the input of those two tools is the same, the
strategy applied is slightly different.
Lets consider a simple example, and see what Vulture does for this example.

First we have a class, which have a single method and vulture will find
all defined names, so these are Foo, spam. Then vulture will find all 
names, which are being used, which are foo and spam. As a last step
it will report all defined names, which are not used.

In this case we end up with an empty set.
So, Nothing will be reported.


Lets complicate this example a little bit by adding additional class called Bar. 
which has a method with the same name.
So, In this case Vulture will first find all defined names: foo spam and bar
Find all used names: foo, spam, bar
And report all defined names, which are not used, which is an empty set.
Because spam method was used once, but vulture does not track namespace of a
those methods. So single usage will mark both spam definitions as used.

So Bar.spam method wont be reported, even though it should be.

Lets consider a different strategy by deadcode: and deadcode uses
dot separated paths to track namespaces, where code items are being used.
As a first step deadcode will find defined names.
In this case spam method will have two occourances, we will foo.spam and bar dot spam.

So Those are two different definitions.
As a second step, we will detect that Foo.spam method is being used.
But no entry for Bar dot spam.

as a last step we will find that Bar.spam was defined, but not used.
In this case deadcode will detect this code occourance correctly.

It is really hard to track the type of a variable, which is being used.
because this variable might be passed into different context and sometimes
dot separated namespace will be lost. 

In such cases vulture will fallback to vultures strategy and a single
occourance will be found.


We came to a conclusion, that deadcode package prevents technical debt
from apperaing out of unused code.

So this should save time, money and reduce some risks.

Thank you for listening? And do you have any questions?


















